/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  phasechange01_partial_condenser  --  a partial condenser

  A superheated benzene/toluene vapour enters a condenser that is asked to
  land its outlet at a vapour QUALITY of 0.4 (40 % of the moles stay vapour,
  60 % condense).  The duty Q is the RESULT -- the unit solves it as a
  built-in self-target (no outerDict, no variables block) and ANNOUNCES that
  it did so.  Because the mixture is multicomponent, the condensed liquid is
  toluene-RICH and the remaining vapour benzene-RICH -- the partial-
  condensation lesson: a partial condenser is also a SEPARATION.

      feedVapour (superheated, 1 bar, 390 K, vf = 1)
        -> condenser (outletQuality 0.4)
        -> wetOut (two-phase, 0 < vf < 1)

  Same outlet vocabulary as rankine02_water's IF97 boiler/condenser, on the
  GENERIC (Antoine + Raoult) backend.

  A NOTE THIS CASE EARNED THE HARD WAY (2026-09-12).  The line above has said
  "feedVapour (superheated, 1 bar, 390 K, vf = 1)" since the case was written,
  and `0/feedVapour` did not say it -- the file declared T and P and no phase.
  Prose is not a channel the engine reads.  So `phaseChanger` took vf = 0, and
  a unit whose entire subject is condensation published Q_latent_kW = +370.98:
  a POSITIVE latent term, with the sensible term carrying -1001.49 to make the
  total duty come out right.  The total was right, both halves were wrong, and
  the plant's first law was out by -898.64 kW at exit 0 -- pinned at 142.526 %
  in `check_energy_closure` and looked at by nobody.  `0/feedVapour` now
  carries `phase gas;`, the run closes at 0.0000 kW, and that pin is GONE
  rather than re-measured.  Record: docs/design/the-word-that-was-not-there.md
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        condenser;
        type        condenser;            // alias of phaseChanger
        in          feedVapour;
        outputs     ( wetOut );

        operation
        {
            outletQuality  0.4;            // 40 % vapour -> Q is the result
        }
    }
);
